    10	  sources (and over in-process tuple relations) associatively — by content, never by
    11	  address.
    12	
    13	Storage holds facts at rest; the tuple space is those facts *under one specific
    14	interpretation*: exact associative tuple matching. DaoSpace is one point in
    15	Datom.world's broader moduli space of database interpreters (see
    16	[DaoSpace Is One Point](#daospace-is-one-point)).
    17	A tuple space is defined by two complementary moves: how agents **read** and how they
    18	**write**. Reading is **associative matching** — you locate a tuple by describing its
    19	*content*, never by naming its address. Writing is **generative communication** — you deposit
    20	a tuple into the shared medium and never address a receiver. Take the read side first; its
    21	surfaces form a spectrum along the by-content axis:
    22	
    23	- **`match`** — a single positional tuple template, Linda-style. One template, matched by
    24	  content, returns the tuples that fit. This is associative matching in its most basic form.
    25	- **`q` (Datalog)** — the *same* by-content matching **generalized to conjunctions**: many
    26	  templates joined on shared logic variables, plus negation, recursion, and aggregation. A
    27	  single-clause `q` *is* a `match`; Datalog keeps the associativity and generalizes
    28	  the arity from one tuple to many tuples joined. So `match ⊂ q`, both associative.
    29	- **Graph / tree / entity-centric / columnar traversal** — these locate data by **following a
    30	  reference or position**. That is navigation, not matching: addressing, not by-content. They
    31	  are useful *local read ergonomics* over an interpreter's own materialized views, but they sit
    32	  *off* the associative axis.
