   354	## The Write Path
   355	
   356	The read side is matching; the write side is how datoms get into the shared medium. A datom
   357	enters by being appended to a writer-owned `dao.stream` — an append-only log the writer owns
   358	and never edits in place. Opening a log is what makes a writer a participant: it attaches a
   359	feeding stream to the space; closing it detaches. This is the mechanics behind the
   360	generative-communication move described in the intro (deposit by appending, name no
   361	recipient) and behind the decentralized Transactor of [Three Boundaries](#three-boundaries).
   362	
   363	### Intake is explicit topology, not identity
   364	
   365	Two explicit topologies must stay distinct, because they sit on different sides of the
   366	storage boundary (`dao.jing.md`, *Physical intake versus semantic composition*):
   367	
   368	- **DaoJing intake pools** — the streams a DaoJing observer materializes into content
   369	  storage. Physical ingestion topology.
   370	- **Query pools** — the published index descriptors a reader currently opens. Semantic composition:
   371	  an explicit collection of DaoStream descriptors.
   372	
   373	Pools may change at runtime by producing a new collection, while each published manifest
   374	and its materialized history persist independently. Storage, [`dao.jing`](dao.jing.md),
   375	remains the dumb materialization; it never interprets or registers pool membership.
   376	
   377	Because each writer owns a single-writer log, two agents never write the same stream. If
   378	1,000 agents want to send messages to one recipient, they append to 1,000 distinct
   379	single-writer streams, and the recipient merges them on the read side — no shared write
   380	surface, no contention.
   381	
   382	### Indexing is the writer's duty
   383	
   384	The write path does not end at the append. In Datomic the transactor also builds the covered
   385	indexes and saves them to storage; here that duty is decentralized with the rest of the
   386	Transactor. Indexing has two stages, mirroring Datomic's memory-index → disk-index pipeline
   387	but without a central transactor process:
