   408	### Fault Tolerance (Crash-Only Semantics)
   409	
   410	Because the write path uses persistent append-only `dao.stream` files, the space inherits
   411	crash-only semantics natively:
   412	
   413	- **Data safety:** Datoms flushed before a crash are safe; append-only files have no
   414	  partial-update corruption window.
   415	- **Reader behavior:** A reader tailing a crashed writer's stream simply reaches the end and
   416	  yields (`ds/next` returns `:blocked`); it waits for new data rather than failing.
   417	- **Write recovery:** A restarted writer reopens its file in append mode; the next `ds/append!`
   418	  lands safely after the last flushed datom.
   419	- **Read recovery:** A reader resumes from a checkpointed cursor, so an incremental index
   420	  rebuilds without reprocessing or skipping. A checkpoint persists the whole cursor map
   421	  (`(:cursor result)`, not just a bare `:position` offset when the transport's cursors carry
   422	  more); a cursor that has fallen behind a retention boundary returns `:daostream/gap`, and
   423	  resynchronization is the caller's decision. Because `publish-index!` never rewrites the
   424	  local stream — it appends to a separate intake stream — publishing cannot reorder what a
   425	  local-stream cursor walks.
