    25	## Definition
    26	
    27	A `dao.jing` observes an explicit pool of intake `dao.stream` values and
    28	materializes their elements into content-addressed key-value storage.
    29	
    30	For every opaque payload `x` arriving through any stream in the pool, DaoJing
    31	performs the same storage operation:
    32	
    33	```text
    34	bytes = canonical-encode(x)
    35	key   = content-hash(bytes)
    36	KV[key] = x                 ; insert if absent
    37	```
    38	
    39	DaoJing knows how to:
    40	
    41	- observe the streams in its configured pool;
    42	- retain the operational cursor needed to continue reading each stream;
    43	- canonically encode an element;
    44	- derive its content address;
    45	- insert it idempotently into the materialization target; and
    46	- retrieve content by its address.
    47	
    48	DaoJing does not know whether an element is a datom, B-tree node, covered
    49	index, manifest, program, image, or any other kind of value. Meaning belongs
    50	to the interpreter that produced or consumes the content.
    51	
    52	This makes DaoJing suitable storage for **any point in Datom.world's moduli
    53	space of database interpreters**. DaoSpace can store covered indexes and
    54	indexed snapshots in it; DaoField can store metric indexes; another interpreter
    55	can store graph, document, columnar, model, or other materializations. All are
    56	opaque values at this boundary.
    57	
    58	DaoJing is itself an interpretation of DaoStream, but only at the
    59	representation level: it interprets each emitted value as content to encode,
    60	hash, and materialize in a key/value store. That operation assigns content
    61	identity and retrieval semantics while remaining devoid of domain semantics.
    62	DaoJing is therefore shared storage beneath the database moduli space, not a
    63	semantic database point within it.
    64	
    65	DaoJing maintains no membership registry, no mutable roots, no CAS records,
    66	and no delete operation. Its only object of discourse is the strict content
    67	address `:segment/sha256-<hash>`, and its only semantics are insert-if-absent
    68	materialization plus content reads.
    69	
    70	## The intake pool
    71	
    72	The pool contains the streams through which content is submitted for
    73	materialization. It is supplied explicitly when the observer state is
    74	constructed. DaoJing performs no stream registration or discovery and
    75	maintains no shared membership record.
    76	
    77	The pool is an intake topology, not a semantic namespace. DaoJing does not use
    78	the identity of the source stream when deriving an element's key, and it does
    79	not attach source identity to the stored value:
    80	
    81	```text
    82	stream A emits x ─┐
    83	                  ├── hash(canonical-encode(x)) ──► the same KV entry
    84	stream B emits x ─┘
    85	```
    86	
    87	Identical content therefore converges on the same address regardless of which
    88	pool member carried it. Pool order, source identity, and arrival order do not
    89	change content identity.
    90	
    91	DaoJing must distinguish pool members operationally long enough to maintain a
    92	cursor for each one. That association is observer state only. It is not
    93	written into the content address or materialized payload and has no semantic
    94	meaning. In the implemented observer each member entry is plain data —
    95	`{:stream <ref>, :cursor {:position n}, :status s}` — an operational record,
    96	nothing more.
    97	
    98	If a storage backend partitions content into physical buckets or shards, that
    99	placement is a backend concern, normally derived from the content hash. It is
   100	not derived from the source stream.
   101	
   102	## Publication from an agent
   103	
   104	An agent writes ordinary working data to its local `dao.stream`. That local
   105	stream is not automatically a member of DaoJing's intake pool.
   106	
   107	When the agent calls `dao.space.index/publish-index!`, the index publisher:
   108	
   109	1. reads the agent's local stream;
   110	2. constructs the covered indexes;
   111	3. selects an intake stream from the DaoJing pool;
   112	4. appends the immutable index payloads, including the top-level manifest, to
   113	   that intake stream; and
   114	5. returns the manifest and its content address required by the publishing
   115	   layer.
   116	
   117	```text
   118	agent-local dao.stream
   119	        │
   120	        │ dao.space.index/publish-index!
   121	        ▼
   122	selected DaoJing intake stream
   123	        │
   124	        │ opaque payloads
   125	        ▼
   126	DaoJing observer ── canonical encode + hash ──► content-addressed KV
   127	```
   128	
   129	Selecting an intake stream is an operational routing decision. It does not
   130	become part of an index payload's identity. The same index payload published
   131	through a different intake stream receives the same address.
   132	
   133	Publication acknowledges that every payload was appended to the selected
   134	intake stream. It does not acknowledge that an asynchronous observer has yet
   135	materialized those payloads; see *Open items and current limitations*.
   136	
   137	Publication changes representation and access cost, not the meaning of the
   138	covered data. Consumers whose explicit read coordinates name the published
   139	manifest address can consume the persisted covered indexes.
   140	
   141	## Materialization rule
   142	
   143	The materialization is an idempotent union of content-addressed values. If
   144	`H(x)` is the address of the canonical encoding of `x`, observing `x` means:
