   201	
   202	## Physical intake versus semantic composition
   203	
   204	Two forms of multi-stream work must remain separate:
   205	
   206	- **Physical intake:** DaoJing observes every stream in its configured pool and
   207	  content-addresses their opaque elements into the KV materialization.
   208	- **Semantic composition:** `dao.space.query` receives an explicit collection
   209	  of published sources and decides how their covered datoms are folded and
   210	  interpreted.
   211	
   212	DaoJing owns the first operation. It has no knowledge of the second. Observing
   213	many intake streams does not mean that DaoJing semantically merges their
   214	contents.
   215	
   216	## Reads
   217	
   218	The storage read operation resolves a strict content address to the exact
    58	outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by identity in the
    59	data; neither side holds a reference to the other.
    60	
    61	An adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an
    62	interpreter. It isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the
    63	effect never appears as an emission.
    64	
    65	A callback is an event on a stream. That is the same rule in the other
    66	direction: when the host initiates -- a socket receives, a connection drops, a
    67	timer fires -- the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes
    68	nothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.
    69	
    70	Constructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event
    71	to data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.
    72	
    73	**An adapter is a map of those functions. That is all an adapter is.** It is a
    74	value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files, timers and windows
    75	all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is what they call. The
    76	host library is handed the map. Each entry takes the host's arguments and
    77	returns one value; the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry
    78	cannot return a value to the host and cannot invoke anything, because it is
    79	handed nothing to invoke. Host types are classified into plain data here -- a
    80	transform is the last place holding a host error object, and no host type may
    81	cross onto the stream.
    82	
    83	Which stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the
    84	adapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one
    85	transport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read, and it does
    86	not need to. This is what keeps the layering one-way: a boundary depends on
    87	nothing above it, because whoever constructs the adapter supplies the stream.
    88	
    89	A transform deposits everything and judges nothing. Data is syntax (axiom 2), so
    90	worth is not a property of the datum -- an event that is noise to one interpreter
    91	is signal to another that does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing
    92	layer destroys the perspectives that have not been taken. How much is retained is
    93	the medium's retention policy, which is operational; what an event means is
    94	interpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.
    95	
    96	An adapter that takes a function to invoke has relocated the callback, not
    97	removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data. An adapter whose
    98	callback has a meaningful return value is worse: that is a synchronous call in
    99	both directions wearing the shape of an event.
   100	
   101	Neither direction needs a vocabulary of its own. Host events are ordinary
   102	values on an ordinary stream, so they need no bespoke envelope, version field,
   103	or fact taxonomy to be read.
   104	
   105	Host error types never cross the boundary. A transform function classifies them
   106	and emits a qualified value.
   107	
   108	A host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported
   109	result. That is a correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.
   110	
   111	## Design Principles
   112	
   113	Code quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?
   114	Stigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.
   115	
    55	| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |
    56	| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |
    57	| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |
    58	| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |
    59	| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |
    60	
    61	A handle implements the subset of these operations its transport
    62	declares (see Surfaces).
    63	
    64	## Invariants
    65	
    66	- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.
    67	- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.
    68	- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.
    69	- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.
    70	- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.
    71	- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase
    72	  retained history.
    73	- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap
    74	  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.
    75	- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.
    76	- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is
    77	  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.
    78	
    79	Every clause below derives from these invariants. A proposed addition that
    80	cannot be derived from one of them does not belong in this contract.
    81	
    82	## Result Convention
    83	
    84	Every DaoStream operation returns data.
    85	
   220	
   221	- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the
   222	  dispatch key.
   223	
   224	Every other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace
   225	(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler
   226	— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its
   227	own keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.
   228	
   229	The **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object
   230	reference. Its exact definition is not designed yet; these properties are
   231	settled, and the key set that realizes them is TBD:
   232	
   233	- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch
   234	  key.
   235	- It names exactly one logical stream, and that identity is stable: the same
   236	  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.
   237	- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is
   238	  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is
   239	  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns
   240	  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.
   241	- Like an IOR, it may carry authorization: an optional `:dao.stream/shibi`
   242	  key holding Shibi capability tokens that gate whether an interpreter may
   243	  attach. Shibi is not yet designed or built; until it is, the key is
   244	  reserved, no attachment is gated, and the `:dao.stream/denied` outcome
   245	  reserved in the `attach!` table below is unreachable.
   246	
   247	For the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`
   248	on a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,
   249	a value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end,
   250	where `attach!` on the reconstructed value reaches the stream it names —
   251	provided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and
   252	Attachment on reachability).
   253	
   254	Both envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive
   255	the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is
   256	structural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or
   257	identity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —
   258	a consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.
   259	
   260	`create!` outcomes:
   261	
   262	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |
   263	|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   264	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists.                  | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   265	| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |
   266	| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |
   267	| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |
   268	
   269	`attach!` outcomes:
   270	
   271	| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |
   272	|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   273	| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | Attached to the existing logical stream.                                            | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   274	| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |
   275	| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |
   330	transport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per
   331	its declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly
   332	defect. (`dao.stream.ws.md` specifies one such transport and the admission
   333	rule in detail.)
   334	
   335	A transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it
   336	excludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is
   337	impossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never
   338	reports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer
   339	evicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a reported `gap` and never
   340	as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport
   341	that cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An
   342	exclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.
   343	
   344	### Where the asynchrony goes
   345	
   346	The IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous
   347	whether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in
   348	a callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does
   349	not pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's
   350	boundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what
   351	arrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and
   352	every reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,
   353	when it chooses.
   354	
   355	This is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but
   356	that the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a
   357	retained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary
   358	code to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing
   359	stated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,
   360	and its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.
    38	## The Duplex Model
    39	
    40	A WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the
    41	design keeps them separate:
    42	
    43	- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and
    44	  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.
    45	- **Inbound**: each arriving message is a host event. The host's adapter
    46	  deposits it — payloads and connection lifecycle events alike, judging
    47	  nothing — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this
    48	  boundary. Per ADR 0003 there is no separate event bus: dao.space is the
    49	  medium. From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,
    50	  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that
    51	  stream.
    52	
    53	The deposit destination is wired once, when the host composes the boundary
    54	— it is not a parameter of `attach!`, and no attaching caller chooses or
    55	knows it. Every interpreter takes its own perspective on the same deposited
    56	truth: one indexes the traffic, another copies it to a file, a third ignores
    57	it. How arrivals are interpreted is the interpreter's business, not the
    58	transport's configuration.
    59	
    60	**Attachment identity.** A descriptor names the served stream, so it cannot
    61	distinguish two attachments to it, and a handle is a host-local object that
    62	cannot be compared against data that crossed a codec. So a ws `attach!`
    63	success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` — a structurally serializable
    64	value, unique per attachment — and every event deposited for that attachment
    65	carries the same value. Without it a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence
    66	with no demultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and
    67	unattributable.
    68	
    69	**Deposited events are envelopes**, so that payload and lifecycle are
    70	distinguishable and both are attributable:
    71	
    72	```clojure
    73	{:dao.stream/attachment <id>
    74	 :ws/event              :ws/payload  ; or :ws/opened :ws/closed :ws/error
    75	                                     ;    :ws/not-found :ws/transport-error
    76	 :ws/value              <decoded>}   ; payload events only
    77	```
    78	
    79	Wrapping is not judging — the adapter never looks inside `:ws/value`. Nothing
    80	of this crosses the wire: the receiving side already knows which socket
    81	delivered a message, so correlation is added locally, on both ends
    82	independently, and the wire carries bare payloads.
    83	
    84	**Granularity is the composition's choice.** One medium per boundary
    85	multiplexes every attachment onto one sequence and one retention window, so a
    86	flood from one attachment evicts another's events. One medium per attachment
    87	isolates them, but the boundary must then announce new attachments on a
    88	boundary-level lifecycle stream, since nothing discovers a stream by itself.
    89	Either shape carries the identity; only the sharing changes.
    90	
    91	```clojure
    92	(def sock (-> (ws/attach! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws
    93	                           :ws/host "example.org"
    94	                           :ws/port 9090
    95	                           :ws/path "/streams/telemetry"})
    96	              :dao.stream/handle))
    97	
    98	;; write: (append! sock msg)
    99	;; read:  interpreters observe the medium the host boundary deposits into,
   100	;;        filtering on this attachment's identity
   101	```
   102	
   103	Nothing accumulates inside the transport: a hidden per-socket inbox would
   104	be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed, sized by
   105	nobody. Retention lives in the medium the boundary deposits into, composed
   106	and sized by the host.
   107	
   108	There are no callback hooks. The legacy `on-open!` / `on-message!` /
   158	## Envelope
   159	
   160	Transport-owned keys, qualified under `:ws/…`. The exact key set is TBD
   161	alongside the portable descriptor's definition in the contract; settled
   162	properties:
   163	
   164	- The descriptor is self-contained and exists to cross the network: it
   165	  carries what any host needs to reach the served stream — host and port,
   166	  plus the served stream's identity (a path or stream id; endpoint address
   167	  alone does not name a stream), perhaps TLS parameters.
   168	- The descriptor carries identity only. Where inbound traffic lands is the
   169	  host boundary's composition (see The Duplex Model) and appears in no
   170	  envelope and no argument of `attach!`.
   171	
   172	## The Handshake
   173	
   174	Attaching presents the served stream's identity, and the endpoint either
   175	accepts it or **authoritatively disclaims** it. Both halves are wire protocol
   176	specified here, not per-composition: a client transport must recognize the
   177	disclaimer to deposit `:ws/not-found` rather than a generic failure, so a
   178	composition inventing its own would not interoperate with any client. What a
   179	composition owns is only which identities it serves — its resolution table —
   180	and never the vocabulary of asking and disclaiming.
   181	
   182	## Operations
   183	
   184	- There is no `create!`. The transport owns no stream, so it creates none;
   185	  a host serves a stream it composed elsewhere, and starting an endpoint is
   186	  a host composition step, not a DaoStream operation. A creation
   187	  specification naming this transport returns `:dao.stream/not-found`.
   188	- `attach!` begins connecting and returns without waiting, per the
   189	  contract's rule that no operation waits. It answers what is decidable
   190	  locally, at the moment it is called: `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` for
   191	  a descriptor it cannot read, otherwise `:dao.stream/ok` with a handle on
   192	  an attachment still being established. It never returns an answer it has
   193	  not heard. This is not a concession to convenience: on a host with no
   194	  blocking IO the endpoint's answer cannot arrive before `attach!` returns,
   195	  and an `attach!` that waited for it would have to hand back a promise —
   196	  breaking the result convention on three of four hosts and giving the same
   197	  transport different outcome sets per host.
   198	- **How the connection resolved arrives as data**, on the same host-composed
   199	  medium as every other socket event, correlated by attachment identity.
   200	  The distinction survives intact, only its channel moves:
   201	  `:dao.stream/not-found` is the authoritative answer that what the
   202	  descriptor names does not exist — the endpoint responded and disclaimed
   203	  the stream — and is not retried automatically (though a server may serve
   204	  that identity later; retrying on external evidence is interpreter
   205	  policy), while `:dao.stream/transport-error` covers transient
   206	  reachability and connection failures — DNS, timeout, routing, refusal —
   207	  and retrying it may succeed on its own. A caller's retry policy is as
   208	  deterministic as before; it reads the answer from the medium instead of
   209	  from a return value.
   210	- `append!` returns `:dao.stream/full` when the send buffer cannot accept
    20	Correcting that raises the real question. If interpreters must find traces without knowing
    21	their source, the traces need a shared medium — and the obvious name for it is a system event
    22	bus.
    23	
    24	## Decision
    25	
    26	**There is no system event bus. `dao.space` is the medium.**
    27	
    28	datom.world is a tuple space because the tuple space is the formal model of stigmergy; the
    29	lineage is blackboard → Linda → `dao.space` (`dao.space.md` § Lineage). A system event bus
    30	would therefore be a *second* stigmergic medium standing beside the one the project exists to
    31	be, with its own retention, its own addressing, and its own set of interpreters — duplicating
    32	the blackboard rather than using it.
    33	
    34	Host boundaries deposit into `dao.space`. Interpreters read it, and deposit their derived
    35	facts back into it at a higher level of abstraction.
    36	
    37	## Consequences
    38	
    39	- **No new mechanism is required, on either side.** `dao.space.transactor/DaoStreamLog`
    40	  already satisfies `dao.stream`'s `IDaoStreamWriter`, and its `-append!` accepts an entity
    41	  map or datom vector and expands it to datoms. An adapter transform returning an ordinary
    42	  map is already a legitimate deposit.
    43	
    44	- **The layering stays one-way, and this is why.** `dao.space` requires `dao.stream`;
    45	  `dao.stream` requires nothing from `dao.space`. A boundary that *named* `dao.space` would
    46	  invert that. It does not need to: an adapter is handed a stream and cannot tell what is
    47	  behind it, so the composition happens above, where something already knows about both
    48	  layers. The generality of the adapter definition is what preserves the layering.
    49	
    50	- **Volume and durability are retention questions, not admission questions.** Data is syntax
    51	  (axiom 2), so no depositing layer may filter by presumed worth. The operational answer
    52	  lives with the selected medium's transport, which declares its capacity and retention
    53	  policy (evict-oldest versus reject); `dao.stream` standardizes only the outcomes —
    54	  `:dao.stream/full`, `:dao.stream/gap` — so loss is never silent and a reader is never
    55	  silently repositioned.
    56	
    57	- **The medium must answer with data.** An adapter entry runs on a host callback thread, where
    58	  a thrown exception has nowhere to go, and `dao.stream`'s axiom is that all expected
    59	  operational outcomes are data. `dao.space.transactor`'s writer face does not yet satisfy
    60	  this: `-append!` throws on a closed wrapper, on invalid input, and on a refused inner
    61	  append — including `:dao.stream/full`, which is ordinary backpressure. Fixing that writer
    62	  face is a precondition of depositing through it.
    63	
    64	- **A rejected alternative, recorded because it recurs.** Splitting deposits by kind — events
    65	  to the medium, high-volume payload kept private — is the same error as the private ingress,
    66	  in different clothes: a depositing layer deciding which data deserves the medium. The
    67	  distinction that does survive is addressing, not worth: the contents of a channel resolved
    68	  by name are asked for, not discovered.
    69	
    70	## Open Questions
    71	
    72	- Whether a connection's payload stream is a deposit or an addressed channel, given that the
    73	  link protocol depends on its positional semantics.
    74	- Retention policy for host-boundary deposits at socket volume.
    75	- Whether `dao.space`'s own `transact!` / `append!` public API should also answer with data,
    25	   descriptor round trip across a real serialization boundary, Model A
    26	   serving, writer-only surfaces, deposit admission, and observable
    27	   teardown. Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, RPC) wait until
    28	   this slice stands.
    29	
    30	The slice still requires a minimal reader-surface core — the served stream
    31	and the deposit destination are both ring buffers — so the ring buffer is
    32	in scope, but only what the contract requires of it, nothing the legacy
    33	one happens to have.
    34	
    35	## Settled before Phase 1
    36	
    37	Three questions a phase would otherwise be forced to invent under delivery
    38	pressure. Each is decided here so no implementer decides it alone.
    39	
    40	**`attach!` is synchronous and never waits.** The Result Convention makes
    41	every operation return an outcome map; the contract's IO Model generalizes
    42	it — no operation waits. No transport in this slice returns a promise, a
    43	future, or a host-native async value from any of the seven operations, on
    44	any host. A ws `attach!` answers what is locally decidable (`ok`,
    45	`invalid-descriptor`) and how the connection resolved arrives as deposited
    46	lifecycle data and through the handle's own `append!` outcomes; see
    47	`dao.stream.ws.md`'s Operations section, corrected to match. A promise
    48	leaking out of a v2 operation is a defect, not a host accommodation.
    49	
    50	The rule covers `create!` equally, though this slice never exercises it:
    51	the ring buffer is in-memory and creates synchronously on every host, and
    52	the ws transport has no `create!` at all — it owns no stream, and starting
    53	an endpoint is a host composition step. A later transport whose creation
    54	needs host resources it cannot acquire synchronously (binding a port,
    55	opening a handle through an async-only API) answers the same way `attach!`
    56	does: the logical stream's identity exists at once, the handle reports
    57	acquisition state through its own operations, and the result of the
    58	acquisition arrives as data. Nothing waits.
    59	
    60	**Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated.** The contract's
    61	public surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy, and must
    62	not gain one — a predicate answer about a live handle is exactly what
    63	`closed?` was retired for. So the boundary constructor takes the deposit
    64	destination *and its declared retention nature as data*, derived from the
    65	same creation specification that produced the handle, and refuses a
    66	reject-mode declaration at assembly. This is configuration provenance, not
    67	runtime introspection. Runtime behavior is unchanged: every non-`ok`
    68	deposit result tears the connection down.
    69	
    70	**Logical-stream identity is minted in Phase 2.** `cursor-mismatch` in
    71	Phase 2 and stable descriptor identity in Phase 3 are the same identity.
    72	It is allocated when a logical stream is created, and both cursors and
    73	descriptors denote it. Their *representations* need not match and need not
    74	be equally serializable — cursor serialization across hosts stays TBD per
    75	the contract — but they must refer to the same thing.
